  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   - Central idea: We will implement a multi-resolution optimization strategy that iteratively improves candidate solutions across different grid resolutions (28, 56, 112, 224, 280). This approach efficiently transfers structure between grids while maintaining domain alignment and integral constraints.\n   - Rationale: The problem's non-convex nature and high-dimensional solution space require exploring multiple resolutions to escape local optima. Coarser grids provide better structure extraction, while finer grids enable precise correlations. By leveraging repair functions and multi-start optimization, we enhance global search capability.\n   - Implementation will include:\n     * Adapted SLSQP with repair mechanism\n     * Momentum-based projected gradient with warm restarts\n     * Coarse-to-fine grid transfer with interpolation and upsampling\n     * Strict feasibility checks to satisfy sum(h)=n/2 exactly\n   - Potential limitation: Requires maintaining separate solver states for each grid resolution. Requires careful interpolation/upsampling.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Objective: Minimize C5 = (2/np) * max_k np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')[k]\n   - Constraints:\n     * 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n     * sum(h) = n_samples / 2 exactly (integral constraint)\n   - Data representation:\n     * Grid points: even spacing [0, 2] with n = n_samples\n     * Correlation parameterized by shift k\n   - Repair function ensuring feasible representations while preserving structure\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Function: run(seed=42, budget_s=1100)**kwargs)\n     * Initialization:\n        1. Create parent grid chains with repair alignment\n        2. Set optimization parameters (max 5 optimization attempts per grid)\n        3. Allocate solution storage (grid size, candidate, score)\n     * Optimization loop:\n        a. From 280->112->56->28->(then upsample to 224)\n        b. Align grid structure with interpolation/upsampling\n        c. For each grid:\n            i. Initialize from parent solution or random start\n            ii. Run SLSQP with repair for n_opt=1000 params for grid n_s\n            iii. Run momentum-based projected gradient with n_opt=500 params\n            iv. Apply repair to all found candidates\n        d. Select best candidate across all grids\n     * Final solution selection:\n        * Track minimum C5 across all grids\n        * Include solution details (h_values, c5_value, n_points)\n     * Budget management:\n        * Cost estimates: Optimization ~ O(n_points) per grid level\n        * Graceful degradation: Increase steps if fallback available\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - Core configuration:\n     * Base grid: 280 samples\n     * Progressive grids: 112, 56, 28, 224 (upsampling)\n     * Iterations: 1000 (SLSQP) + 500 (gradient)\n     * Repair tolerance: 1e-6 (exact integral constraint)\n   - Adaptive repairs:\n     * Clip values to [0,1]\n     * Scale to meet integral constraint\n     * Multi-stage repairs when needed\n   - Variation: Use initial_h_values from task environment\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Major costs:\n     * Objective eval: O(n_points) per call\n     * SLSQP optimization: O(n_params) ~ O(n_points)\n     * Grid size mapping: O(n_points*log(n_points))\n     * Interpolation/repair: O(n_points)\n     * Analysis: Coarse grids (28->112->224) are cheaper than finest (280)\n   - Suggested schedule:\n     * 60% budget for largest grid optimization\n     * 40% for remaining grids (lower-dimensional)\n   - Fallback: Given initial_h_values, use adaptation if no progress\n   - Handling failures:\n     * If repair fails: Scale values by target/sum\n     * If correlation undefined: Clip unused regions\n     * If SLSQP fails: Resort to steepest descent\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Feasibility checks:\n     * Repair h to [0,1] and sum=n/2\n     * Check overlap integral via np.correlate\n     * Verify objective value before/after repair\n   - Return format:\n     * h_values: numpy array (float64) of length n_points\n     * c5_bound: float value of objective\n     * n_points: integer sample count\n   - Custom checks included:\n     * Integral maintenance in repair\n     * Valid domain alignment\n     * Objective agreement between grids\n   - Timing safeguards:\n     * Monitor elapsed time before optimization\n     * Early termination if exceeds budget",
